# The inputs of docker-compose.production.yml (D-94). Copy to production.env, fill in the empty
# values, keep the file private (it holds secrets), then:
#
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.production.yml --env-file production.env up -d --wait
#   scripts/production-init.sh
#
# Every variable is explained in docs/CONFIG.md, which owns them. A test keeps this file and the
# compose file in step: a required variable is set below and left EMPTY when it is a secret, so a
# file nobody edited stops `docker compose` naming the first one, rather than starting a stack
# with a default password. Optional variables are commented with their default.

# --- required -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The API image: a digest (ghcr.io/<owner>/purlay-api@sha256:...) or a commit tag. Never a moving tag.
PURLAY_IMAGE=
# The one public https origin, no path. It is also the proxy's site address.
APP_URL=https://chat.example.com
# URL-safe, used inside DATABASE_URL:            openssl rand -hex 16
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
# 64 hex characters:                             openssl rand -hex 32
GARAGE_RPC_SECRET=
# Garage's admin API, which prepares the store on `up` (inside the stack only): openssl rand -hex 32
GARAGE_ADMIN_TOKEN=
# Garage's own key format, GK + 24 hex digits:   echo GK$(openssl rand -hex 12)
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# 64 hex characters:                             openssl rand -hex 32
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# 32 random bytes, base64. Losing it makes stored integration tokens unreadable; keep it
# separately from backups:                       openssl rand -base64 32
ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# --- optional (defaults shown) --------------------------------------------------------------------

# LOG_LEVEL=info
# The GitHub OAuth app, both or neither, read on the first start only. Leave them out and the server
# starts in setup mode: the setup page in the browser connects GitHub. Its callback URL is
# ${APP_URL}/auth/callback/github.
# GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
# GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Single sign-on by an OpenID Connect provider (D-200), all three or none, read on the first start only;
# or connect it later in Administration → Server. Redirect URI to register: ${APP_URL}/auth/callback/oidc.
# OIDC_ISSUER=
# OIDC_CLIENT_ID=
# OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=
# OIDC_NAME=
# DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10
# SESSION_TTL_DAYS=30
# A GitHub Enterprise Server instead of github.com: its address, and its API's (https://host/api/v3).
# GITHUB_URL=https://github.com
# GITHUB_API_URL=https://api.github.com
# Who may make a workspace: GitHub logins, comma-separated, or * for anybody signed in. Unset, only
# the first person to sign in may, while there is none; everyone else is invited (D-155). Read on the
# first start only, as are the GitHub app and the mail settings: after it, Administration → Server.
# WORKSPACE_CREATORS=
# A Sentry-compatible DSN; unset means nothing is reported.
# ERROR_TRACKING_DSN=
# S3_REGION=us-east-1
# S3_BUCKET=purlay-attachments
# Web Push: all three or none; none is push off, and the in-app inbox is still the record. Generate
# the pair with `bun run push:keys` in a checkout, or with the image itself:
#   docker run --rm <PURLAY_IMAGE> apps/api/src/cli/push-keys.ts
# and copy both lines as printed. The private key is a secret. The
# subject is mailto: an address, or the https: address of this deployment.
# VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
# VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
# VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:ops@example.com
# Email sign-in: both or neither; neither is GitHub sign-in only. smtps://user:pass@host:465, or
# smtp:// on 587 with STARTTLS required unless SMTP_ALLOW_PLAINTEXT=true says the link is private.
# SMTP_URL=
# MAIL_FROM=
# SMTP_ALLOW_PLAINTEXT=false
# The lowest an installed macOS client may be and still be served; none set is no limit. A client below
# either is told to update, with the address to get a newer one from (https only). docs/CONFIG.md.
# MIN_CLIENT_MACOS_CONTRACT=
# MIN_CLIENT_MACOS_VERSION=
# MACOS_DOWNLOAD_URL=
# Host ports of the proxy. The HTTPS port must also be in APP_URL (https://host:8443); a certificate
# from a public CA needs 80 and 443 reachable from outside, so move them only behind another proxy
# or with certificates of your own.
# HTTP_PORT=80
# HTTPS_PORT=443
# Memory bounds of the containers (docs/spikes/resources.md, #639): several times what each was
# measured to use. Raise one on a larger server; a container that reaches its bound is restarted,
# the host is not taken with it.
# API_MEMORY_LIMIT=768m
# SCHEDULER_MEMORY_LIMIT=512m
# STORAGE_INIT_MEMORY_LIMIT=256m
# POSTGRES_MEMORY_LIMIT=1536m
# GARAGE_MEMORY_LIMIT=512m
# PROXY_MEMORY_LIMIT=256m
